      "source": [
        {
          "question": "e-6731",
          "field": null,
          "attribute": "question/problem",
          "value": "The function $y(t)$ gives the position of a particle at time $t > 0.$ Determine whether each equation is forced or free, and whether it is damped.\n\n- $y''+y'+3y = t$\n\n- $y''+ 4y = e^t$\n\n- $y''+y'+3y = 0$",
          "category": "ma_capture",
          "file": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065932/example-6731.json",
          "file_sha256": "7cf186f226028ed6a709aa690eac86b54b97571e6943bbaaa5994588bb91d9e4"
        }
      ],
      "basis": 2165,
      "reason": "Observed MA content supersedes the stored value",
      "action": "replace",
      "transaction_receipt": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065932/edb-import/commit.edn",
      "basis_after": 2166
    },
    {
      "question": "e-6731",
      "field": null,
      "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
      "old_sha256": "fb8fb726584784cc0a7c1113cc6e73b3fea578234e16c26cecd52befda40ed7d",
      "new_sha256": "b036d1f9c404c8c435a4774bde1fe2121e93bbb308769cc5dda17bfcb7d52558",
      "category": "ma_capture",
      "authoring": [],
      "source": [
        {
          "question": "e-6731",
          "field": null,
          "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
          "value": "The differential equation\n\n$$\ny'' + b y' + \\omega^2 y = f(t)\n$$\n\nis forced if $f(t)$ is not identical to zero, and damped if $b>0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's classify each equation.\n\n- Equation I represents forced harmonic motion with damping. The forcing function $f(t)=t$ is not always zero, and there is a damping term with $b=1>0.$\n\n- Equation II represents forced harmonic motion without damping. The forcing function $f(t)=e^t$ is not always zero, but there is no damping term since $b=0.$\n\n- Equation III represents free (non-forced) harmonic motion with damping. The forcing function $f(t)=0$ is always zero, and there is a damping term with $b=1>0.$",
          "category": "ma_capture",
          "file": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065932/example-6731.json",
          "file_sha256": "7cf186f226028ed6a709aa690eac86b54b97571e6943bbaaa5994588bb91d9e4"
        }
      ],
      "basis": 2165,
      "reason": "Observed MA content supersedes the stored value",
      "action": "replace",
      "transaction_receipt": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065932/edb-import/commit.edn",
      "basis_after": 2166
    },
    {
      "question": "e-6732",
      "field": null,
      "attribute": "question/problem",
      "old_sha256": "949ff2cbd067ee6b8f3e8d995aa9f3ed571fb0ac57b3d26bf0580975697ffa31",
      "new_sha256": "d22049deb14c375b666e8a0d35a4886ad72373c37899a64b220b0aa5545a84d7",
      "category": "ma_capture",
      "authoring": [],
      "source": [
        {
          "question": "e-6732",
          "field": null,
          "attribute": "question/problem",
          "value": "A particle of mass $3\\,\\textrm{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. The spring constant associated with the spring is $k = 6\\, \\textrm{kg/s}^2.$  The particle is also subject to the resistive force that's proportional to the particle's velocity with a damping coefficient $c = 9\\, \\textrm{kg/s},$ and an external force given by $F(t)=3t^2\\,\\textrm{N}.$   \n\nThe position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds.  If the particle moves with forced harmonic motion, what is the governing equation?",
          "category": "ma_capture",
          "file": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065932/example-6732.json",
          "file_sha256": "5e10db39e5c040e140cab42b1a266daa034728979cf3f26c81998a5419de4c68"
        }
      ],
      "basis": 2165,
      "reason": "Observed MA content supersedes the stored value",
      "action": "replace",
      "transaction_receipt": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065932/edb-import/commit.edn",
      "basis_after": 2166
    },
    {
      "question": "e-6732",
      "field": null,
      "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
      "old_sha256": "b334b7d08815398993ef5c6bde8d07e3a41c36eb552ed627d07fd9919b293c22",
      "new_sha256": "052adc0cfb5279a0448076d2120d5c69268dc3cad9f0fe1bff52103d73290a58",
      "category": "ma_capture",
      "authoring": [],
      "source": [
        {
          "question": "e-6732",
          "field": null,
          "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
          "value": "The general equation of forced harmonic motion with damping is\n\n$$\nm y'' =-cy' -k y + F(t) .\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $m,$ $k,$ $c$ and $F(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n3 y'' =-9y' -6 y+3t^2.\n$$\n\nSimplifying and rearranging the above equation, we get\n\n$$\ny'' + 3y'+2y = t^2.\n$$",
          "category": "ma_capture",
          "file": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065932/example-6732.json",
          "file_sha256": "5e10db39e5c040e140cab42b1a266daa034728979cf3f26c81998a5419de4c68"
        }
      ],
      "basis": 2165,
      "reason": "Observed MA content supersedes the stored value",
      "action": "replace",
      "transaction_receipt": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065932/edb-import/commit.edn",
      "basis_after": 2166
    },
    {
      "question": "e-6733",
      "field": null,
      "attribute": "question/problem",
      "old_sha256": "2659a99b40df805913c2bf0f9e10a2e4eb05fd5b9884558b56123d687f661d2a",
      "new_sha256": "51ba981ade35952e8bd0c8242a6494a134a81adedb506eaabce4f7ef81d74e55",
      "category": "ma_capture",
      "authoring": [],
      "source": [
        {
          "question": "e-6733",
          "field": null,
          "attribute": "question/problem",
          "value": "A particle is moving with forced harmonic motion with damping. The position of the particle $y(t)$ at time $t$ is governed by the equation\n\n$$\ny''+2y' + 5 y = 50t.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be used to represent the general solution for $y(t)?$\n\n- $y =Ae^{-t}+Be^{-2t}+25t+5$\n\n- $y = R e ^ {- t} \\cos \\left( 2t + \\phi \\right) +10t - 4$\n\n- $y =e^{-t}\\left( A\\cos{2t} + B\\sin{2t}\\right)+10 t-4$\n\nNote that $A$ and $B$ are constants.",
          "category": "ma_capture",
          "file": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065932/example-6733.json",
          "file_sha256": "11ed26f4beba67672eb305f2d2bfd9d8b988bc25f25e17c113c0988df5bf3228"
        }
      ],
      "basis": 2165,
      "reason": "Observed MA content supersedes the stored value",
      "action": "replace",
      "transaction_receipt": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065932/edb-import/commit.edn",
      "basis_after": 2166
    },
    {
      "question": "e-6733",
      "field": null,
      "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
      "old_sha256": "59cdb3fefb2247ccfb3e1eaebeffd6e3d65d02dc5506acd40c09be30185bf88a",
      "new_sha256": "394db5b4717f294d242d373546a7d3937be780b3052da653c010cb4cfbbdc430",
      "category": "ma_capture",
      "authoring": [],
      "source": [
        {
          "question": "e-6733",
          "field": null,
          "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
          "value": "The general solution is the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution:\n\n$$\ny(t) = y_c(t)+y_p(t)\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the complementary solution $y_c(t),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation $y''+2y' + 5 y = 0.$ Writing down and solving the characteristic equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda +5 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda +1)-1+5 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)^{2}+4 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)^{2} & =-4 \\\\ \\lambda +1 & =\\pm 2\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =-1\\pm 2\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the complementary function $y_c(t)$ is can be expressed in any of the following forms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{c}(t) & ={e}^{-t}(A\\operatorname{cos}\u20612t+B\\operatorname{sin}\u20612t), \\\\ {y}_{c}(t) & =R{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(2t+\u03d5), \\\\ {y}_{c}(t) & =R{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(2t+\u03d5).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the particular solution $y_p(t).$ Note that the external force $f(t)=50t$ is a linear function. Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a linear function,\n\n$$\ny_p(t)=\\alpha t+\\beta.\n$$\n\nBy substituting the particular solution into the differential equation and equating coefficients, we find that $\\alpha = 10$ and $\\beta = -4.$ So, the particular solution is\n\n$$\ny_p(t)= 10 t - 4.\n$$\n\nFinally, then, the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary and particular solutions. So, it can be written in any of the following forms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & ={e}^{-t}(A\\operatorname{cos}\u20612t+B\\operatorname{sin}\u20612t)+10t-4, \\\\ y(t) & =R{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(2t+\u03d5)+10t-4, \\\\ y(t) & =R{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(2t+\u03d5)+10t-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOnly II and III match the forms above. Therefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only\".",
          "category": "ma_capture",
          "file": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065932/example-6733.json",
          "file_sha256": "11ed26f4beba67672eb305f2d2bfd9d8b988bc25f25e17c113c0988df5bf3228"
        }
      ],
      "basis": 2165,
